WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.369
Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award-winning

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:05.371
actress you know from films
such as "The Danish Girl,"

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:07.741
"Tomb Raider," and "Ex Machina."
She stars next in "The Glorias,"

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:09.709
which will be released
on September 30th

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:11.477
on Amazon Prime Video
and Digital.

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:12.912
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:15.148
-This year, the press
has finally discovered

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:19.652
a movement that has already been
strong for several years now,

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:22.187
and reported it
as a small, privileged,

00:00:22.187 --> 00:00:24.124
rather lunatic event,

00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:26.526
instead of a major evolution
in consciousness.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:30.563
-Everyone's consciousness, male
and female, black and white.

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:34.867
This is the year
of women's liberation!

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.370
-Please welcome back to the show
Alicia Vikander.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.938
How are you?

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:41.206
-I'm good.
Thank you for having me.

00:00:41.206 --> 00:00:43.375
-It's wonderful
to see you again.

00:00:43.375 --> 00:00:45.045
Very good Gloria Steinem voice.

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:46.645
I have to give you
full credit there.

00:00:46.645 --> 00:00:48.147
That was very impressive.

00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:50.316
-[Deep voice] Yeah, I had to --
I mean, I do have a low voice.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.952
[Normal voice, laughing]
But I had to go even lower.

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:56.089
-Yeah.
-This is a wonderful film

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:58.758
where different actors
play Gloria Steinem

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.660
over the course of her life

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.595
based on a book
she wrote about her life.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:07.099
And you and Julianne Moore
actually got to meet her.

00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:08.935
I was lucky enough
to have her on this show.

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:11.971
I mean, she is a titan,
and it's very --

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.806
I was nervous talking to her.

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:15.741
Were you even more nervous
knowing that you then

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:18.244
had to take on
the task of playing her?

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:19.712
-Yeah, I was.
It was almost like --

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:23.649
I had this question asked
to me just earlier today --

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:26.085
"Oh, how was it meeting her
the first time?"

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:27.386
And I suddenly remembered,

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:29.889
I did actually meet her
for lunch first

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.190
before we met for rehearsals.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.093
But I think I had almost
had that as a blank

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:37.497
because I was probably
so nervous.

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:39.832
I was just so kind of --
you know, felt so privileged

00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:42.235
and honored
to just be able to meet her.

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:45.838
And, of course,
I managed to be a bit more calm

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:48.674
when she saw me, Julianne,
and Julie, our director,

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:50.309
for rehearsals,

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:52.678
which she was so generous
to tell me

00:01:52.678 --> 00:01:54.080
that she could be part of.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.684
I think it was, yeah,
quite a few times

00:01:57.684 --> 00:02:00.753
when Julianne and I
just looked at one another,

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:02.821
couldn't really believe
what was happening.

00:02:02.821 --> 00:02:07.727
But Gloria, she was --
she's so incredibly generous,

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:10.362
and she kind of
pushed us and said,

00:02:10.362 --> 00:02:13.566
"You know, please,
just ask me anything."

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:17.803
And in the end, we actually --
I was a bit embarrassed to ask,

00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:22.608
but she actually read
some of the scenes

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:25.144
from the script herself,
as herself,

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:26.412
which was pretty mind-blowing.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.314
I even --
I actually have some videos

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.883
still on my iPhone,
which is a bit of a treasure.

00:02:30.883 --> 00:02:33.052
-That's an incredible
advantage you got.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.221
When I used to have to do
impressions on

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:37.289
"Saturday Night Live,"
I'd have to record it off the TV

00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:38.992
to actually get the person
to do it for you.

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:40.626
-Yeah, I mean,
it was one of those things.

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:43.296
I was like, "Is it okay?
Can I film you doing this?"

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:44.864
And she was like,
"Yeah, of course."

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:46.599
And, you know,
we ended up getting like,

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:47.966
I don't know, 20 videos.

00:02:47.966 --> 00:02:50.503
And I think, in the end,
I think she kind of enjoyed it,

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:52.338
but it was -- We each
kind of started the scenes,

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.607
and then she was like --
stopped halfway.

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:56.709
And then she was like,
"Well, actually

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:58.277
what happened at that event,"

00:02:58.277 --> 00:03:00.579
or, "Actually what I did say
or what that person said."

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:02.414
And then it would always
turn out

00:03:02.414 --> 00:03:04.383
to a much longer,
exciting story.

00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:05.852
-That is fantastic.

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:07.420
You know, I think
the interesting thing

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.488
about Gloria's life
is she's always been timely,

00:03:09.488 --> 00:03:12.525
and yet it seems
more timely than ever

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:15.828
to be making this movie now.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:19.331
You guys must have been aware of
that while you were making it.

00:03:19.331 --> 00:03:22.167
-Of course.
I mean, a lot of the discussions

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:26.038
that we had whilst filming
was how excited we were,

00:03:26.038 --> 00:03:28.274
you know,
for the release of the film

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:30.976
and, of course, the topics
that it brings up.

00:03:30.976 --> 00:03:33.178
And the fact that Gloria,
you know,

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:35.348
from the beginning
has been so supportive

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:40.119
and wanted to be part of us
releasing this film.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:41.854
And we had big plans,
so, you know,

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:47.927
hoping to be on tour now
and be on a bus and involve,

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:52.832
you know, a lot of other women,
do a lot of talking circles,

00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:56.268
which is something
that Gloria does a lot of.

00:03:56.268 --> 00:03:58.370
Now, of course, the world
looks very different,

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:04.210
but it was a very beautiful
kind of communal feel

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.712
that we got a few weeks ago
when we --

00:04:05.712 --> 00:04:09.348
I think we were, like,
16 women on a Zoom call.

00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:10.949
Gloria was one of them.

00:04:10.949 --> 00:04:13.218
We kind of passed around,
you know, the questions

00:04:13.218 --> 00:04:15.087
of "What can you do?"

00:04:15.087 --> 00:04:18.124
And everyone had
their own ideas of how --

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:20.859
how we can kind of
get the message out.

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:22.861
-It's --
You talk about how hard it is

00:04:22.861 --> 00:04:24.530
to have those communal moments,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.566
but I have heard
that one solution

00:04:26.566 --> 00:04:30.236
you have to the dinner party
that we can no longer have

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:32.705
is to order the same take-out

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.907
as the people
that you Zoom with.

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.475
This is a very, very novel idea.

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:38.243
-It started
as something I did --

00:04:38.243 --> 00:04:40.179
you know, like, a sneaky thing
I can -- you know, to a friend,

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.915
I can kind of sent
a text and like --

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:45.818
It happened once when
my friend was stateside,

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:47.620
so it was, like, a Sunday.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.322
So it ended up
being a dinner for me

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:51.391
and a brunch for my friend.

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:53.759
And I was like,
"Yeah, this is an address.

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:55.428
You need to be there
in half an hour."

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:58.564
And then I had
just called, like, another --

00:04:58.564 --> 00:04:59.998
I think it was
a Mexican restaurant.

00:04:59.998 --> 00:05:01.967
And the good thing is,
like, Mexican food, you know,

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:05.438
you can kind of decide to order
the same thing wherever you are.

00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:09.608
So then we ended up having
the exact same, you know, order,

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:12.645
and I think we even had
the same bottle of wine.

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:14.480
And then, you know,
that's a great way

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.615
of really forgetting

00:05:16.615 --> 00:05:19.285
about the fact that you are
in front of a screen.

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:20.820
The tough thing is, though,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.356
because if you've had
a few glasses of wine,

00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:26.158
suddenly you have
this amazing social event --

00:05:26.158 --> 00:05:29.695
Suddenly you just, like,
go offline,

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:32.064
and then you feel really lonely.

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:33.666
-[Fingers snap]
-Suddenly, everything's gone.

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:35.401
-Right, and then nobody
believes you when you say,

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:36.902
"Look, I was at dinner
with a friend."

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:39.071
They're like, "Were you?"

00:05:39.071 --> 00:05:41.173
You -- I want
to congratulate you,

00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:44.376
as well, 'cause you got this
in right under the wire.

00:05:44.376 --> 00:05:47.947
You got your driver's license
in the UK.

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:49.381
-I did!

00:05:49.381 --> 00:05:52.484
I mean, literally a few days
before lockdown.

00:05:52.484 --> 00:05:57.055
I mean, it's been --
It's been way too -- too long.

00:05:57.055 --> 00:05:59.158
I promised myself I was
going to do it before 30,

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:00.559
but that didn't happen.

00:06:00.559 --> 00:06:03.762
But now I have it, and,
you know, I was born in Sweden,

00:06:03.762 --> 00:06:05.564
which drive on the right side,

00:06:05.564 --> 00:06:08.868
so for me to have passed
the test in the UK,

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:11.204
in London, too,
with all the black cabs,

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:13.339
and everything
and on the left side,

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:17.377
I feel like I'm hopefully
not a huge danger

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:19.578
when I go out there
in the traffic.

00:06:19.578 --> 00:06:20.980
-I think the good news is,

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.515
no matter
how bad a driver you are,

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:24.217
there are so many
other huge dangers

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:25.885
right now that you're so --

00:06:25.885 --> 00:06:28.187
you're comfortably
at the bottom of the list.

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:30.489
-People will forget
if I just run over them.

00:06:30.489 --> 00:06:32.057
-Yeah, if you sideswipe
somebody right now,

00:06:32.057 --> 00:06:33.392
that's the least
of their problems.

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:35.394
And it's really good now
I think, more than ever,

00:06:35.394 --> 00:06:37.029
a good time
to be able to get in a car

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:38.330
and drive as fast as possible.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:40.499
So congratulations
on your license.

00:06:40.499 --> 00:06:42.434
Congratulations, as well,
on the film.

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:44.069
It's always so nice to see you.

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:46.739
-So good to see you.
Thank you so much.

